<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05SY">Remark <span data-tag="05SY">7.7.1.12</span> <span class="named">(Cartesian Transformations as Cartesian Sections)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $\mathscr {G}: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a diagram, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} K$ denote the oriented fiber product of Definition <a data-tag="01KF" href="/tag/01KF">4.6.4.1</a>. Then projection onto the second factor determines a cocartesian fibration of simplicial sets $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} K \rightarrow K$. By construction, sections of $U$ can be identified with pairs $(\mathscr {F}, \gamma )$, where $\mathscr {F}: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a diagram and $\gamma : \mathscr {F} \rightarrow \mathscr {G}$ is a natural transformation. In this case, the natural transformation $\gamma $ is cartesian (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05SM" href="/tag/05SM">7.7.1.1</a>) if and only if the corresponding section of $U$ is cartesian: that is, it carries each edge of $K$ to a $U$-cartesian edge of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} K$. This is a reformulation of Proposition <a data-tag="05S7" href="/tag/05S7">7.6.2.31</a>. </p>
</article>
